# Which diagnostic codes are allowed to be WARNINGS. Hand-maintained,
# and that is the entire point: everything else in this directory is
# written by the compiler, so an invariant read off a golden is an
# invariant the compiler under test decides.
#
# It was not hypothetical. An adversary flipped one token in
# `typecheck.ax` - `SEV_ERROR` to `SEV_WARNING` on AX3004 - re-blessed
# the AXDL goldens from the resulting compiler, and this gate passed:
# 55 checks, 0 failed, every span still verified. That compiler exits 0
# on a type-incorrect program and emits LLVM for it, including a call
# through an `inttoptr` of an integer. A guaranteed segfault, shipped as
# a successful build, and green.
#
# Re-run with this file in place, in a shadow tree so the real one was
# never touched: the re-bless still succeeds - it has to, it is only
# writing down what the compiler said - and the gate then exits 1 with
#
#     FAIL severity: AX3004 renders as a WARNING in a golden but is
#     not in tests/diagnostics/severity.policy
#
# which is the drill this file exists to pass.
#
# The reason the gate could not see it is that the severity letter in
# the golden and the exit status are the SAME field: `countErrorDiags`
# and `sevSigil` both read `(memGetWord d 0)`, which docs/diagnostics.md
# records as the design. So "the golden says E, therefore the run exits
# non-zero" is a theorem about the compiler's source rather than a test
# of it. This file breaks the circle by being the one artifact here that
# no compiler writes.
#
# One code per line. A code absent from this list must never appear as
# `W` in any `.axdl` golden.
#
# AX3010 - an AXTAG that claims an effect the function does not have.
#          A claim that is merely wrong about code that compiles.
AX3010
# AX3037 - an AXTAG claim the effect walk cannot check, because the
#          body calls a value it could not resolve. A limit of the
#          ANALYSIS rather than a defect in the program - the code
#          compiles and may well be correct - so it informs and does
#          not refuse. The same reasoning as AX3010 one line up, and
#          the same standing risk: if this ever becomes the compiler's
#          answer to a program that is actually wrong, it belongs in
#          the other list.
AX3037
